<p align=3D"CENTER">
<b>BERKSHIRE HATHAWAY INC.</b>
</p>

<pre>

<b>To the Shareholders of Berkshire Hathaway Inc.:</b>

     Our gain in net worth during 1984 was $152.6 million, or=20
$133 per share.  This sounds pretty good but actually it=92s=20
mediocre.  Economic gains must be evaluated by comparison with=20
the capital that produces them.  Our twenty-year compounded=20
annual gain in book value has been 22.1% (from $19.46 in 1964 to=20
$1108.77 in 1984), but our gain in 1984 was only 13.6%.

     As we discussed last year, the gain in per-share intrinsic=20
business value is the economic measurement that really counts. =20
But calculations of intrinsic business value are subjective.  In=20
our case, book value serves as a useful, although somewhat=20
understated, proxy.  In my judgment, intrinsic business value and=20
book value increased during 1984 at about the same rate.

     Using my academic voice, I have told you in the past of the=20
drag that a mushrooming capital base exerts upon rates of return.=20
Unfortunately, my academic voice is now giving way to a=20
reportorial voice.  Our historical 22% rate is just that -=20
history.  To earn even 15% annually over the next decade=20
(assuming we continue to follow our present dividend policy,=20
about which more will be said later in this letter) we would need=20
profits aggregating about $3.9 billion.  Accomplishing this will=20
require a few big ideas - small ones just won=92t do.  Charlie=20
Munger, my partner in general management, and I do not have any=20
such ideas at present, but our experience has been that they pop=20
up occasionally. (How=92s that for a strategic plan?)

