     Sharp-eyed shareholders will notice that the amount of the=20
special GEICO distribution and its location in the table have=20
been changed from the presentation of last year.  Though they=20
reclassify and reduce =93accounting=94 earnings, the changes are=20
entirely of form, not of substance.  The story behind the=20
changes, however, is interesting.

     As reported last year: (1) in mid-1983 GEICO made a tender=20
offer to buy its own shares; (2) at the same time, we agreed by=20
written contract to sell GEICO an amount of its shares that would=20
be proportionately related to the aggregate number of shares=20
GEICO repurchased via the tender from all other shareholders; (3)=20
at completion of the tender, we delivered 350,000 shares to=20
GEICO, received $21 million cash, and were left owning exactly=20
the same percentage of GEICO that we owned before the tender; (4)=20
GEICO=92s transaction with us amounted to a proportionate=20
redemption, an opinion rendered us, without qualification, by a=20
leading law firm; (5) the Tax Code logically regards such=20
proportionate redemptions as substantially equivalent to=20
dividends and, therefore, the $21 million we received was taxed=20
at only the 6.9% inter-corporate dividend rate; (6) importantly,=20
that $21 million was far less than the previously-undistributed=20
earnings that had inured to our ownership in GEICO and, thus,=20
from the standpoint of economic substance, was in our view=20
equivalent to a dividend.

     Because it was material and unusual, we highlighted the=20
GEICO distribution last year to you, both in the applicable=20
quarterly report and in this section of the annual report. =20
Additionally, we emphasized the transaction to our auditors,=20
Peat, Marwick, Mitchell &amp; Co. Both the Omaha office of Peat=20
Marwick and the reviewing Chicago partner, without objection,=20
concurred with our dividend presentation.

     In 1984, we had a virtually identical transaction with=20
General Foods.  The only difference was that General Foods=20
repurchased its stock over a period of time in the open market,=20
whereas GEICO had made a =93one-shot=94 tender offer.  In the General=20
Foods case we sold to the company, on each day that it=20
repurchased shares, a quantity of shares that left our ownership=20
percentage precisely unchanged.  Again our transaction was=20
pursuant to a written contract executed before repurchases began. =20
And again the money we received was far less than the retained=20
earnings that had inured to our ownership interest since our=20
purchase.  Overall we received $21,843,601 in cash from General=20
Foods, and our ownership remained at exactly 8.75%.

     At this point the New York office of Peat Marwick came into=20
the picture.  Late in 1984 it indicated that it disagreed with=20
the conclusions of the firm=92s Omaha office and Chicago reviewing=20
partner.  The New York view was that the GEICO and General Foods=20
transactions should be treated as sales of stock by Berkshire=20
rather than as the receipt of dividends.  Under this accounting=20
approach, a portion of the cost of our investment in the stock of=20
each company would be charged against the redemption payment and=20
any gain would be shown as a capital gain, not as dividend=20
income.  This is an accounting approach only, having no bearing=20
on taxes: Peat Marwick agrees that the transactions were=20
dividends for IRS purposes.

     We disagree with the New York position from both the=20
viewpoint of economic substance and proper accounting.  But, to=20
avoid a qualified auditor=92s opinion, we have adopted herein Peat=20
Marwick=92s 1984 view and restated 1983 accordingly.  None of this,=20
however, has any effect on intrinsic business value: our=20
ownership interests in GEICO and General Foods, our cash, our=20
taxes, and the market value and tax basis of our holdings all=20
remain the same.

     This year we have again entered into a contract with General=20
Foods whereby we will sell them shares concurrently with open=20
market purchases that they make.  The arrangement provides that=20
our ownership interest will remain unchanged at all times.  By=20
keeping it so, we will insure ourselves dividend treatment for=20
tax purposes.  In our view also, the economic substance of this=20
transaction again is the creation of dividend income.  However,=20
we will account for the redemptions as sales of stock rather than=20
dividend income unless accounting rules are adopted that speak=20
directly to this point.  We will continue to prominently identify=20
any such special transactions in our reports to you.

     While we enjoy a low tax charge on these proportionate=20
redemptions, and have participated in several of them, we view=20
such repurchases as at least equally favorable for shareholders=20
who do not sell.  When companies with outstanding businesses and=20
comfortable financial positions find their shares selling far=20
below intrinsic value in the marketplace, no alternative action=20
can benefit shareholders as surely as repurchases.

     (Our endorsement of repurchases is limited to those dictated=20
by price/value relationships and does not extend to the=20
=93greenmail=94 repurchase - a practice we find odious and repugnant. =20
In these transactions, two parties achieve their personal ends by=20
exploitation of an innocent and unconsulted third party.  The=20
players are: (1) the =93shareholder=94 extortionist who, even before=20
the ink on his stock certificate dries, delivers his =93your-
money-or-your-life=94 message to managers; (2) the corporate=20
insiders who quickly seek peace at any price - as long as the=20
price is paid by someone else; and (3) the shareholders whose=20
money is used by (2) to make (1) go away.  As the dust settles,=20
the mugging, transient shareholder gives his speech on =93free=20
enterprise=94, the muggee management gives its speech on =93the best=20
interests of the company=94, and the innocent shareholder standing=20
by mutely funds the payoff.)

     The companies in which we have our largest investments have=20
all engaged in significant stock repurhases at times when wide=20
discrepancies existed between price and value.  As shareholders,=20
we find this encouraging and rewarding for two important reasons=20
- one that is obvious, and one that is subtle and not always=20
understood.  The obvious point involves basic arithmetic: major=20
repurchases at prices well below per-share intrinsic business=20
value immediately increase, in a highly significant way, that=20
value.  When companies purchase their own stock, they often find=20
it easy to get $2 of present value for $1.  Corporate acquisition=20
programs almost never do as well and, in a discouragingly large=20
number of cases, fail to get anything close to $1 of value for=20
each $1 expended.

     The other benefit of repurchases is less subject to precise=20
measurement but can be fully as important over time.  By making=20
repurchases when a company=92s market value is well below its=20
business value, management clearly demonstrates that it is given=20
to actions that enhance the wealth of shareholders, rather than=20
to actions that expand management=92s domain but that do nothing=20
for (or even harm) shareholders.  Seeing this, shareholders and=20
potential shareholders increase their estimates of future returns=20
from the business.  This upward revision, in turn, produces=20
market prices more in line with intrinsic business value.  These=20
prices are entirely rational.  Investors should pay more for a=20
business that is lodged in the hands of a manager with=20
demonstrated pro-shareholder leanings than for one in the hands=20
of a self-interested manager marching to a different drummer. (To=20
make the point extreme, how much would you pay to be a minority=20
shareholder of a company controlled by Robert Wesco?)

     The key word is =93demonstrated=94.  A manager who consistently=20
turns his back on repurchases, when these clearly are in the=20
interests of owners, reveals more than he knows of his=20
motivations.  No matter how often or how eloquently he mouths=20
some public relations-inspired phrase such as =93maximizing=20
shareholder wealth=94 (this season=92s favorite), the market=20
correctly discounts assets lodged with him.  His heart is not=20
listening to his mouth - and, after a while, neither will the=20
market.

     We have prospered in a very major way - as have other=20
shareholders - by the large share repurchases of GEICO,=20
Washington Post, and General Foods, our three largest holdings.=20
(Exxon, in which we have our fourth largest holding, has also=20
wisely and aggressively repurchased shares but, in this case, we=20
have only recently established our position.) In each of these=20
companies, shareholders have had their interests in outstanding=20
businesses materially enhanced by repurchases made at bargain=20
prices.  We feel very comfortable owning interests in businesses=20
such as these that offer excellent economics combined with=20
shareholder-conscious managements.

