     It=92s been over ten years since it has been as difficult as=20
now to find equity investments that meet both our qualitative=20
standards and our quantitative standards of value versus price. =20
We try to avoid compromise of these standards, although we find=20
doing nothing the most difficult task of all. (One English=20
statesman attributed his country=92s greatness in the nineteenth=20
century to a policy of =93masterly inactivity=94.  This is a strategy=20
that is far easier for historians to commend than for=20
participants to follow.)

     In addition to the figures supplied at the beginning of this=20
section, information regarding the businesses we own appears in=20
Management=92s Discussion on pages 42-47.  An amplified discussion=20
of Wesco=92s businesses appears in Charlie Munger=92s report on pages=20
50-59.  You will find particularly interesting his comments about=20
conditions in the thrift industry.  Our other major controlled=20
businesses are Nebraska Furniture Mart, See=92s, Buffalo Evening=20
News, and the Insurance Group, to which we will give some special=20
attention here.

<b>Nebraska Furniture Mart</b>

     Last year I introduced you to Mrs. B (Rose Blumkin) and her=20
family.  I told you they were terrific, and I understated the=20
case.  After another year of observing their remarkable talents=20
and character, I can honestly say that I never have seen a=20
managerial group that either functions or behaves better than the=20
Blumkin family.

     Mrs. B, Chairman of the Board, is now 91, and recently was=20
quoted in the local newspaper as saying, =93I come home to eat and=20
sleep, and that=92s about it.  I can=92t wait until it gets daylight=20
so I can get back to the business=94.  Mrs. B is at the store seven=20
days a week, from opening to close, and probably makes more=20
decisions in a day than most CEOs do in a year (better ones,=20
too).

     In May Mrs. B was granted an Honorary Doctorate in=20
Commercial Science by New York University. (She=92s a =93fast track=94=20
student: not one day in her life was spent in a school room prior=20
to her receipt of the doctorate.) Previous recipients of honorary=20
degrees in business from NYU include Clifton Garvin, Jr., CEO of=20
Exxon Corp.; Walter Wriston, then CEO of Citicorp; Frank Cary,=20
then CEO of IBM; Tom Murphy, then CEO of General Motors; and,=20
most recently, Paul Volcker. (They are in good company.)

     The Blumkin blood did not run thin.  Louie, Mrs. B=92s son,=20
and his three boys, Ron, Irv, and Steve, all contribute in full=20
measure to NFM=92s amazing success.  The younger generation has=20
attended the best business school of them all - that conducted by=20
Mrs. B and Louie - and their training is evident in their=20
performance.

     Last year NFM=92s net sales increased by $14.3 million,=20
bringing the total to $115 million, all from the one store in=20
Omaha.  That is by far the largest volume produced by a single=20
home furnishings store in the United States.  In fact, the gain=20
in sales last year was itself greater than the annual volume of=20
many good-sized successful stores.  The business achieves this=20
success because it deserves this success.  A few figures will=20
tell you why.

     In its fiscal 1984 10-K, the largest independent specialty=20
retailer of home furnishings in the country, Levitz Furniture,=20
described its prices as =93generally lower than the prices charged=20
by conventional furniture stores in its trading area=94.  Levitz,=20
in that year, operated at a gross margin of 44.4% (that is, on=20
average, customers paid it $100 for merchandise that had cost it=20
$55.60 to buy).  The gross margin at NFM is not much more than=20
half of that.  NFM=92s low mark-ups are possible because of its=20
exceptional efficiency: operating expenses (payroll, occupancy,=20
advertising, etc.) are about 16.5% of sales versus 35.6% at=20
Levitz.

     None of this is in criticism of Levitz, which has a well-
managed operation.  But the NFM operation is simply extraordinary=20
(and, remember, it all comes from a $500 investment by Mrs. B in=20
1937).  By unparalleled efficiency and astute volume purchasing,=20
NFM is able to earn excellent returns on capital while saving its=20
customers at least $30 million annually from what, on average, it=20
would cost them to buy the same merchandise at stores maintaining=20
typical mark-ups.  Such savings enable NFM to constantly widen=20
its geographical reach and thus to enjoy growth well beyond the=20
natural growth of the Omaha market.

     I have been asked by a number of people just what secrets=20
the Blumkins bring to their business.  These are not very=20
esoteric.  All members of the family: (1) apply themselves with=20
an enthusiasm and energy that would make Ben Franklin and Horatio=20
Alger look like dropouts; (2) define with extraordinary realism=20
their area of special competence and act decisively on all=20
matters within it; (3) ignore even the most enticing propositions=20
failing outside of that area of special competence; and, (4)=20
unfailingly behave in a high-grade manner with everyone they deal=20
with. (Mrs.  B boils it down to =93sell cheap and tell the truth=94.)

     Our evaluation of the integrity of Mrs. B and her family was=20
demonstrated when we purchased 90% of the business: NFM had never=20
had an audit and we did not request one; we did not take an=20
inventory nor verify the receivables; we did not check property=20
titles.  We gave Mrs. B a check for $55 million and she gave us=20
her word.  That made for an even exchange.

     You and I are fortunate to be in partnership with the=20
Blumkin family.

