     This performance has not been produced by a generally rising=20
tide.  To the contrary, many well-known participants in the=20
boxed-chocolate industry either have lost money in this same=20
period or have been marginally profitable.  To our knowledge,=20
only one good-sized competitor has achieved high profitability. =20
The success of See=92s reflects the combination of an exceptional=20
product and an exceptional manager, Chuck Huggins.

     During 1984 we increased prices considerably less than has=20
been our practice in recent years: per-pound realization was=20
$5.49, up only 1.4% from 1983.  Fortunately, we made good=20
progress on cost control, an area that has caused us problems in=20
recent years.  Per-pound costs - other than those for raw=20
materials, a segment of expense largely outside of our control -=20
increased by only 2.2% last year.

     Our cost-control problem has been exacerbated by the problem=20
of modestly declining volume (measured by pounds, not dollars) on=20
a same-store basis.  Total pounds sold through shops in recent=20
years has been maintained at a roughly constant level only by the=20
net addition of a few shops annually.  This more-shops-to-get-
the-same-volume situation naturally puts heavy pressure on per-
pound selling costs.

     In 1984, same-store volume declined 1.1%. Total shop volume,=20
however, grew 0.6% because of an increase in stores. (Both=20
percentages are adjusted to compensate for a 53-week fiscal year=20
in 1983.)

     See=92s business tends to get a bit more seasonal each year. =20
In the four weeks prior to Christmas, we do 40% of the year=92s=20
volume and earn about 75% of the year=92s profits.  We also earn=20
significant sums in the Easter and Valentine=92s Day periods, but=20
pretty much tread water the rest of the year.  In recent years,=20
shop volume at Christmas has grown in relative importance, and so=20
have quantity orders and mail orders.  The increased=20
concentration of business in the Christmas period produces a=20
multitude of managerial problems, all of which have been handled=20
by Chuck and his associates with exceptional skill and grace.

     Their solutions have in no way involved compromises in=20
either quality of service or quality of product.  Most of our=20
larger competitors could not say the same.  Though faced with=20
somewhat less extreme peaks and valleys in demand than we, they=20
add preservatives or freeze the finished product in order to=20
smooth the production cycle and thereby lower unit costs.  We=20
reject such techniques, opting, in effect, for production=20
headaches rather than product modification.

     Our mall stores face a host of new food and snack vendors=20
that provide particularly strong competition at non-holiday=20
periods.  We need new products to fight back and during 1984 we=20
introduced six candy bars that, overall, met with a good=20
reception.  Further product introductions are planned.

     In 1985 we will intensify our efforts to keep per-pound cost=20
increases below the rate of inflation.  Continued success in=20
these efforts, however, will require gains in same-store=20
poundage.  Prices in 1985 should average 6% - 7% above those of=20
1984.  Assuming no change in same-store volume, profits should=20
show a moderate gain.

<b>Buffalo Evening News</b>

     Profits at the News in 1984 were considerably greater than=20
we expected.  As at See=92s, excellent progress was made in=20
controlling costs.  Excluding hours worked in the newsroom, total=20
hours worked decreased by about 2.8%. With this productivity=20
improvement, overall costs increased only 4.9%. This performance=20
by Stan Lipsey and his management team was one of the best in the=20
industry.

     However, we now face an acceleration in costs.  In mid-1984=20
we entered into new multi-year union contracts that provided for=20
a large =93catch-up=94 wage increase.  This catch-up is entirely=20
appropriate: the cooperative spirit of our unions during the=20
unprofitable 1977-1982 period was an important factor in our=20
success in remaining cost competitive with The Courier-Express. =20
Had we not kept costs down, the outcome of that struggle might=20
well have been different.

     Because our new union contracts took effect at varying=20
dates, little of the catch-up increase was reflected in our 1984=20
costs.  But the increase will be almost totally effective in 1985=20
and, therefore, our unit labor costs will rise this year at a=20
rate considerably greater than that of the industry.  We expect=20
to mitigate this increase by continued small gains in=20
productivity, but we cannot avoid significantly higher wage costs=20
this year.  Newsprint price trends also are less favorable now=20
than they were in 1984.  Primarily because of these two factors,=20
we expect at least a minor contraction in margins at the News.

     Working in our favor at the News are two factors of major=20
economic importance:

     (1) Our circulation is concentrated to an unusual degree=20
         in the area of maximum utility to our advertisers. =20
         =93Regional=94 newspapers with wide-ranging circulation, on=20
         the other hand, have a significant portion of their=20
         circulation in areas that are of negligible utility to=20
         most advertisers.  A subscriber several hundred miles=20
         away is not much of a prospect for the puppy you are=20
         offering to sell via a classified ad - nor for the=20
         grocer with stores only in the metropolitan area. =20
         =93Wasted=94 circulation - as the advertisers call it -=20
         hurts profitability: expenses of a newspaper are=20
         determined largely by gross circulation while=20
         advertising revenues (usually 70% - 80% of total=20
         revenues) are responsive only to useful circulation;=20

     (2) Our penetration of the Buffalo retail market is=20
         exceptional; advertisers can reach almost all of their=20
         potential customers using only the News.

     Last year I told you about this unusual reader acceptance:=20
among the 100 largest newspapers in the country, we were then=20
number one, daily, and number three, Sunday, in penetration.  The=20
most recent figures show us number one in penetration on weekdays=20
and number two on Sunday.  (Even so, the number of households in=20
Buffalo has declined, so our current weekday circulation is down=20
slightly; on Sundays it is unchanged.)

     I told you also that one of the major reasons for this=20
unusual acceptance by readers was the unusual quantity of news=20
that we delivered to them: a greater percentage of our paper is=20
devoted to news than is the case at any other dominant paper in=20
our size range.  In 1984 our =93news hole=94 ratio was 50.9%, (versus=20
50.4% in 1983), a level far above the typical 35% - 40%.  We will=20
continue to maintain this ratio in the 50% area.  Also, though we=20
last year reduced total hours worked in other departments, we=20
maintained the level of employment in the newsroom and, again,=20
will continue to do so.  Newsroom costs advanced 9.1% in 1984, a=20
rise far exceeding our overall cost increase of 4.9%.

     Our news hole policy costs us significant extra money for=20
newsprint.  As a result, our news costs (newsprint for the news=20
hole plus payroll and expenses of the newsroom) as a percentage=20
of revenue run higher than those of most dominant papers of our=20
size.  There is adequate room, however, for our paper or any=20
other dominant paper to sustain these costs: the difference=20
between =93high=94 and =93low=94 news costs at papers of comparable size=20
runs perhaps three percentage points while pre-tax profit margins=20
are often ten times that amount.

     The economics of a dominant newspaper are excellent, among=20
the very best in the business world.  Owners, naturally, would=20
like to believe that their wonderful profitability is achieved=20
only because they unfailingly turn out a wonderful product.  That=20
comfortable theory wilts before an uncomfortable fact.  While=20
first-class newspapers make excellent profits, the profits of=20
third-rate papers are as good or better - as long as either class=20
of paper is dominant within its community.  Of course, product=20
quality may have been crucial to the paper in achieving=20
dominance.  We believe this was the case at the News, in very=20
large part because of people such as Alfred Kirchhofer who=20
preceded us.

     Once dominant, the newspaper itself, not the marketplace,=20
determines just how good or how bad the paper will be.  Good or=20
bad, it will prosper.  That is not true of most businesses:=20
inferior quality generally produces inferior economics.  But even=20
a poor newspaper is a bargain to most citizens simply because of=20
its =93bulletin board=94 value.  Other things being equal, a poor=20
product will not achieve quite the level of readership achieved=20
by a first-class product.  A poor product, however, will still=20
remain essential to most citizens, and what commands their=20
attention will command the attention of advertisers.

     Since high standards are not imposed by the marketplace,=20
management must impose its own.  Our commitment to an above-
average expenditure for news represents an important quantitative=20
standard.  We have confidence that Stan Lipsey and Murray Light=20
will continue to apply the far-more important qualitative=20
standards.  Charlie and I believe that newspapers are very=20
special institutions in society.  We are proud of the News, and=20
intend an even greater pride to be justified in the years ahead.

