<b>Errors in Loss Reserving</b>

     Any shareholder in a company with important interests in the=20
property/casualty insurance business should have some=20
understanding of the weaknesses inherent in the reporting of=20
current earnings in that industry.  Phil Graham, when publisher=20
of the Washington Post, described the daily newspaper as =93a first=20
rough draft of history=94.  Unfortunately, the financial statements=20
of a property/casualty insurer provide, at best, only a first=20
rough draft of earnings and financial condition.

     The determination of costs is the main problem.  Most of an=20
insurer=92s costs result from losses on claims, and many of the=20
losses that should be charged against the current year=92s revenue=20
are exceptionally difficult to estimate.  Sometimes the extent of=20
these losses, or even their existence, is not known for decades.

     The loss expense charged in a property/casualty company=92s=20
current income statement represents: (1) losses that occurred and=20
were paid during the year; (2) estimates for losses that occurred=20
and were reported to the insurer during the year, but which have=20
yet to be settled; (3) estimates of ultimate dollar costs for=20
losses that occurred during the year but of which the insurer is=20
unaware (termed =93IBNR=94: incurred but not reported); and (4) the=20
net effect of revisions this year of similar estimates for (2)=20
and (3) made in past years.

     Such revisions may be long delayed, but eventually any=20
estimate of losses that causes the income for year X to be=20
misstated must be corrected, whether it is in year X + 1, or    =20
X + 10.  This, perforce, means that earnings in the year of=20
correction also are misstated.  For example, assume a claimant=20
was injured by one of our insureds in 1979 and we thought a=20
settlement was likely to be made for $10,000.  That year we would=20
have charged $10,000 to our earnings statement for the estimated=20
cost of the loss and, correspondingly, set up a liability reserve=20
on the balance sheet for that amount.  If we settled the claim in=20
1984 for $100,000, we would charge earnings with a loss cost of=20
$90,000 in 1984, although that cost was truly an expense of 1979. =20
And if that piece of business was our only activity in 1979, we=20
would have badly misled ourselves as to costs, and you as to=20
earnings.

     The necessarily-extensive use of estimates in assembling the=20
figures that appear in such deceptively precise form in the=20
income statement of property/casualty companies means that some=20
error must seep in, no matter how proper the intentions of=20
management.  In an attempt to minimize error, most insurers use=20
various statistical techniques to adjust the thousands of=20
individual loss evaluations (called case reserves) that comprise=20
the raw data for estimation of aggregate liabilities.  The extra=20
reserves created by these adjustments are variously labeled=20
=93bulk=94, =93development=94, or =93supplemental=94 reserves.  The goal of=
=20
the adjustments should be a loss-reserve total that has a 50-50=20
chance of being proved either slightly too high or slightly too=20
low when all losses that occurred prior to the date of the=20
financial statement are ultimately paid.

     At Berkshire, we have added what we thought were appropriate=20
supplemental reserves but in recent years they have not been=20
adequate.  It is important that you understand the magnitude of=20
the errors that have been involved in our reserving.  You can=20
thus see for yourselves just how imprecise the process is, and=20
also judge whether we may have some systemic bias that should=20
make you wary of our current and future figures.

