and we don=92t receive interest on the amounts we overpaid.)

     Because our business is weighted toward casualty and=20
reinsurance lines, we have more problems in estimating loss costs=20
than companies that specialize in property insurance. (When a=20
building that you have insured burns down, you get a much faster=20
fix on your costs than you do when an employer you have insured=20
finds out that one of his retirees has contracted a disease=20
attributable to work he did decades earlier.) But I still find=20
our errors embarrassing.  In our direct business, we have far=20
underestimated the mushrooming tendency of juries and courts to=20
make the =93deep pocket=94 pay, regardless of the factual situation=20
and the past precedents for establishment of liability.  We also=20
have underestimated the contagious effect that publicity=20
regarding giant awards has on juries.  In the reinsurance area,=20
where we have had our worst experience in under reserving, our=20
customer insurance companies have made the same mistakes.  Since=20
we set reserves based on information they supply us, their=20
mistakes have become our mistakes.

     I heard a story recently that is applicable to our insurance=20
accounting problems: a man was traveling abroad when he received=20
a call from his sister informing him that their father had died=20
unexpectedly.  It was physically impossible for the brother to=20
get back home for the funeral, but he told his sister to take=20
care of the funeral arrangements and to send the bill to him. =20
After returning home he received a bill for several thousand=20
dollars, which he promptly paid.  The following month another=20
bill came along for $15, and he paid that too.  Another month=20
followed, with a similar bill.  When, in the next month, a third=20
bill for $15 was presented, he called his sister to ask what was=20
going on.  =93Oh=94, she said.  =93I forgot to tell you.  We buried Dad=20
in a rented suit.=94

     If you=92ve been in the insurance business in recent years -=20
particularly the reinsurance business - this story hurts.  We=20
have tried to include all of our =93rented suit=94 liabilities in our=20
current financial statement, but our record of past error should=20
make us humble, and you suspicious.  I will continue to report to=20
you the errors, plus or minus, that surface each year.

     Not all reserving errors in the industry have been of the=20
innocent-but-dumb variety.  With underwriting results as bad as=20
they have been in recent years - and with managements having as=20
much discretion as they do in the presentation of financial=20
statements - some unattractive aspects of human nature have=20
manifested themselves.  Companies that would be out of business=20
if they realistically appraised their loss costs have, in some=20
cases, simply preferred to take an extraordinarily optimistic=20
view about these yet-to-be-paid sums.  Others have engaged in=20
various transactions to hide true current loss costs.

     Both of these approaches can =93work=94 for a considerable time:=20
external auditors cannot effectively police the financial=20
statements of property/casualty insurers.  If liabilities of an=20
insurer, correctly stated, would exceed assets, it falls to the=20
insurer to volunteer this morbid information.  In other words,=20
the corpse is supposed to file the death certificate.  Under this=20
=93honor system=94 of mortality, the corpse sometimes gives itself=20
the benefit of the doubt.

     In most businesses, of course, insolvent companies run out=20
of cash.  Insurance is different: you can be broke but flush. =20
Since cash comes in at the inception of an insurance policy and=20
losses are paid much later, insolvent insurers don=92t run out of=20
cash until long after they have run out of net worth.  In fact,=20
these =93walking dead=94 often redouble their efforts to write=20
business, accepting almost any price or risk, simply to keep the=20
cash flowing in.  With an attitude like that of an embezzler who=20
has gambled away his purloined funds, these companies hope that=20
somehow they can get lucky on the next batch of business and=20
thereby cover up earlier shortfalls.  Even if they don=92t get=20
lucky, the penalty to managers is usually no greater for a $100=20
million shortfall than one of $10 million; in the meantime, while=20
the losses mount, the managers keep their jobs and perquisites.

     The loss-reserving errors of other property/casualty=20
companies are of more than academic interest to Berkshire.  Not=20
only does Berkshire suffer from sell-at-any-price competition by=20
the =93walking dead=94, but we also suffer when their insolvency is=20
finally acknowledged.  Through various state guarantee funds that=20
levy assessments, Berkshire ends up paying a portion of the=20
insolvent insurers=92 asset deficiencies, swollen as they usually=20
are by the delayed detection that results from wrong reporting. =20
There is even some potential for cascading trouble.  The=20
insolvency of a few large insurers and the assessments by state=20
guarantee funds that would follow could imperil weak-but-
previously-solvent insurers.  Such dangers can be mitigated if=20
state regulators become better at prompt identification and=20
termination of insolvent insurers, but progress on that front has=20
been slow.

<b>Washington Public Power Supply System</b>

     From October, 1983 through June, 1984 Berkshire=92s insurance=20
subsidiaries continuously purchased large quantities of bonds of=20
Projects 1, 2, and 3 of Washington Public Power Supply System=20
(=93WPPSS=94).  This is the same entity that, on July 1, 1983,=20
defaulted on $2.2 billion of bonds issued to finance partial=20
construction of the now-abandoned Projects 4 and 5. While there=20
are material differences in the obligors, promises, and=20
properties underlying the two categories of bonds, the problems=20
of Projects 4 and 5 have cast a major cloud over Projects 1, 2,=20
and 3, and might possibly cause serious problems for the latter=20
issues.  In addition, there have been a multitude of problems=20
related directly to Projects 1, 2, and 3 that could weaken or=20
destroy an otherwise strong credit position arising from=20
guarantees by Bonneville Power Administration.

     Despite these important negatives, Charlie and I judged the=20
risks <i>at the time we purchased the bonds and at the prices=20
Berkshire paid</i> (much lower than present prices) to be=20
considerably more than compensated for by prospects of profit.

     As you know, we buy marketable stocks for our insurance=20
companies based upon the criteria we would apply in the purchase=20
of an entire business.  This business-valuation approach is not=20
widespread among professional money managers and is scorned by=20
many academics.  Nevertheless, it has served its followers well=20
(to which the academics seem to say, =93Well, it may be all right=20
in practice, but it will never work in theory.=94) Simply put, we=20
feel that if we can buy small pieces of businesses with=20
satisfactory underlying economics at a fraction of the per-share=20
value of the entire business, something good is likely to happen=20
to us - particularly if we own a group of such securities.

     We extend this business-valuation approach even to bond=20
purchases such as WPPSS.  We compare the $139 million cost of our=20
yearend investment in WPPSS to a similar $139 million investment=20
in an operating business.  In the case of WPPSS, the =93business=94=20
contractually earns $22.7 million after tax (via the interest=20
paid on the bonds), and those earnings are available to us=20
currently in cash.  We are unable to buy operating businesses=20
with economics close to these.  Only a relatively few businesses=20
earn the 16.3% after tax on unleveraged capital that our WPPSS=20
investment does and those businesses, when available for=20
purchase, sell at large premiums to that capital.  In the average=20
negotiated business transaction, unleveraged corporate earnings=20
of $22.7 million after-tax (equivalent to about $45 million pre-
tax) might command a price of $250 - $300 million (or sometimes=20
far more).  For a business we understand well and strongly like,=20
we will gladly pay that much.  But it is double the price we paid=20
to realize the same earnings from WPPSS bonds.

     However, in the case of WPPSS, there is what we view to be a=20
very slight risk that the =93business=94 could be worth nothing=20
within a year or two.  There also is the risk that interest=20
payments might be interrupted for a considerable period of time. =20
Furthermore, the most that the =93business=94 could be worth is about=20
the $205 million face value of the bonds that we own, an amount=20
only 48% higher than the price we paid.

     This ceiling on upside potential is an important minus.  It=20
should be realized, however, that the great majority of operating=20
businesses have a limited upside potential also unless more=20
capital is continuously invested in them.  That is so because=20
most businesses are unable to significantly improve their average=20
returns on equity - even under inflationary conditions, though=20
these were once thought to automatically raise returns.

     (Let=92s push our bond-as-a-business example one notch=20
further: if you elect to =93retain=94 the annual earnings of a 12%=20
bond by using the proceeds from coupons to buy more bonds,=20
earnings of that bond =93business=94 will grow at a rate comparable=20
to that of most operating businesses that similarly reinvest all=20
earnings.  In the first instance, a 30-year, zero-coupon, 12%=20
bond purchased today for $10 million will be worth $300 million=20
in 2015.  In the second, a $10 million business that regularly=20
earns 12% on equity and retains all earnings to grow, will also=20
end up with $300 million of capital in 2015.  Both the business=20
and the bond will earn over $32 million in the final year.)

     Our approach to bond investment - treating it as an unusual=20
sort of =93business=94 with special advantages and disadvantages -=20
may strike you as a bit quirky.  However, we believe that many=20
staggering errors by investors could have been avoided if they=20
had viewed bond investment with a businessman=92s perspective.  For=20
example, in 1946, 20-year AAA tax-exempt bonds traded at slightly=20
below a 1% yield.  In effect, the buyer of those bonds at that=20
time bought a =93business=94 that earned about 1% on =93book value=94=20
(and that, moreover, could never earn a dime more than 1% on=20
book), and paid 100 cents on the dollar for that abominable=20
business.

     If an investor had been business-minded enough to think in=20
those terms - and that was the precise reality of the bargain=20
struck - he would have laughed at the proposition and walked=20
away.  For, at the same time, businesses with excellent future=20
prospects could have been bought at, or close to, book value=20
while earning 10%, 12%, or 15% after tax on book.  Probably no=20
business in America changed hands in 1946 at book value that the=20
buyer believed lacked the ability to earn more than 1% on book. =20
But investors with bond-buying habits eagerly made economic=20
commitments throughout the year on just that basis.  Similar,=20
although less extreme, conditions prevailed for the next two=20
decades as bond investors happily signed up for twenty or thirty=20
years on terms outrageously inadequate by business standards.=20
(In what I think is by far the best book on investing ever=20
written - =93The Intelligent Investor=94, by Ben Graham - the last=20
section of the last chapter begins with, =93Investment is most=20
intelligent when it is most businesslike.=94 This section is called=20
=93A Final Word=94, and it is appropriately titled.)

     We will emphasize again that there is unquestionably some=20
risk in the WPPSS commitment.  It is also the sort of risk that=20
is difficult to evaluate.  Were Charlie and I to deal with 50=20
similar evaluations over a lifetime, we would expect our judgment=20
to prove reasonably satisfactory.  But we do not get the chance=20
to make 50 or even 5 such decisions in a single year.  Even=20
though our long-term results may turn out fine, in any given year=20
we run a risk that we will look extraordinarily foolish. (That=92s=20
why all of these sentences say =93Charlie and I=94, or =93we=94.)

     Most managers have very little incentive to make the=20
intelligent-but-with-some-chance-of-looking-like-an-idiot=20
decision.  Their personal gain/loss ratio is all too obvious: if=20
an unconventional decision works out well, they get a pat on the=20
back and, if it works out poorly, they get a pink slip. (Failing=20
conventionally is the route to go; as a group, lemmings may have=20
a rotten image, but no individual lemming has ever received bad=20
press.)

     Our equation is different.  With 47% of Berkshire=92s stock,=20
Charlie and I don=92t worry about being fired, and we receive our=20
rewards as owners, not managers.  Thus we behave with Berkshire=92s=20
money as we would with our own.  That frequently leads us to=20
unconventional behavior both in investments and general business=20
management.

     We remain unconventional in the degree to which we=20
concentrate the investments of our insurance companies, including=20
those in WPPSS bonds.  This concentration makes sense only=20
because our insurance business is conducted from a position of=20
exceptional financial strength.  For almost all other insurers, a=20
comparable degree of concentration (or anything close to it)=20
would be totally inappropriate.  Their capital positions are not=20
strong enough to withstand a big error, no matter how attractive=20
an investment opportunity might appear when analyzed on the basis=20
of probabilities.

     With our financial strength we can own large blocks of a few=20
securities that we have thought hard about and bought at=20
attractive prices. (Billy Rose described the problem of over-
diversification: =93If you have a harem of forty women, you never=20
get to know any of them very well.=94) Over time our policy of=20
concentration should produce superior results, though these will=20
be tempered by our large size.  When this policy produces a=20
really bad year, as it must, at least you will know that our=20
money was committed on the same basis as yours.

     We made the major part of our WPPSS investment at different=20
prices and under somewhat different factual circumstances than=20
exist at present.  If we decide to change our position, we will=20
not inform shareholders until long after the change has been=20
completed. (We may be buying or selling as you read this.) The=20
buying and selling of securities is a competitive business, and=20
even a modest amount of added competition on either side can cost=20
us a great deal of money.  Our WPPSS purchases illustrate this=20
principle.  From October, 1983 through June, 1984, we attempted=20
to buy almost all the bonds that we could of Projects 1, 2, and=20
3. Yet we purchased less than 3% of the bonds outstanding.  Had=20
we faced even a few additional well-heeled investors, stimulated=20
to buy because they knew we were, we could have ended up with a=20
materially smaller amount of bonds, purchased at a materially=20
higher price. (A couple of coat-tail riders easily could have=20
cost us $5 million.) For this reason, we will not comment about=20
our activities in securities - neither to the press, nor=20
shareholders, nor to anyone else - unless legally required to do=20
so.

     One final observation regarding our WPPSS purchases: we=20
dislike the purchase of most long-term bonds under most=20
circumstances and have bought very few in recent years.  That=92s=20
because bonds are as sound as a dollar - and we view the long-
term outlook for dollars as dismal.  We believe substantial=20
inflation lies ahead, although we have no idea what the average=20
rate will turn out to be.  Furthermore, we think there is a=20
small, but not insignificant, chance of runaway inflation.

     Such a possibility may seem absurd, considering the rate to=20
which inflation has dropped.  But we believe that present fiscal=20
policy - featuring a huge deficit - is both extremely dangerous=20
and difficult to reverse. (So far, most politicians in both=20
parties have followed Charlie Brown=92s advice: =93No problem is so=20
big that it can=92t be run away from.=94) Without a reversal, high=20
rates of inflation may be delayed (perhaps for a long time), but=20
will not be avoided.  If high rates materialize, they bring with=20
them the potential for a runaway upward spiral.

     While there is not much to choose between bonds and stocks=20
(as a class) when annual inflation is in the 5%-10% range,=20
runaway inflation is a different story.  In that circumstance, a=20
diversified stock portfolio would almost surely suffer an=20
enormous loss in real value.  But bonds already outstanding would=20
suffer far more.  Thus, we think an all-bond portfolio carries a=20
small but unacceptable =93wipe out=94 risk, and we require any=20
purchase of long-term bonds to clear a special hurdle.  Only when=20
bond purchases appear decidedly superior to other business=20
opportunities will we engage in them.  Those occasions are likely=20
to be few and far between.

<b>Dividend Policy</b>

     Dividend policy is often reported to shareholders, but=20
seldom explained.  A company will say something like, =93Our goal=20
is to pay out 40% to 50% of earnings and to increase dividends at=20
a rate at least equal to the rise in the CPI=94.  And that=92s it -=20
no analysis will be supplied as to why that particular policy is=20
best for the owners of the business.  Yet, allocation of capital=20
is crucial to business and investment management.  Because it is,=20
we believe managers and owners should think hard about the=20
circumstances under which earnings should be retained and under=20
which they should be distributed.

     The first point to understand is that all earnings are not=20
created equal.  In many businesses particularly those that have=20
high asset/profit ratios - inflation causes some or all of the=20
reported earnings to become ersatz.  The ersatz portion - let=92s=20
call these earnings =93restricted=94 - cannot, if the business is to=20
retain its economic position, be distributed as dividends.  Were=20
these earnings to be paid out, the business would lose ground in=20
one or more of the following areas: its ability to maintain its=20
unit volume of sales, its long-term competitive position, its=20
financial strength.  No matter how conservative its payout ratio,=20
a company that consistently distributes restricted earnings is=20
destined for oblivion unless equity capital is otherwise infused.

     Restricted earnings are seldom valueless to owners, but they=20
often must be discounted heavily.  In effect, they are=20
conscripted by the business, no matter how poor its economic=20
potential. (This retention-no-matter-how-unattractive-the-return=20
situation was communicated unwittingly in a marvelously ironic=20
way by Consolidated Edison a decade ago.  At the time, a punitive=20
regulatory policy was a major factor causing the company=92s stock=20
to sell as low as one-fourth of book value; i.e., every time a=20
dollar of earnings was retained for reinvestment in the business,=20
that dollar was transformed into only 25 cents of market value. =20
But, despite this gold-into-lead process, most earnings were=20
reinvested in the business rather than paid to owners. =20
Meanwhile, at construction and maintenance sites throughout New=20
York, signs proudly proclaimed the corporate slogan, =93Dig We=20
Must=94.)

     Restricted earnings need not concern us further in this=20
dividend discussion.  Let=92s turn to the much-more-valued=20
unrestricted variety.  These earnings may, with equal=20
feasibility, be retained or distributed.  In our opinion,=20
management should choose whichever course makes greater sense for=20
the owners of the business.

     This principle is not universally accepted.  For a number of=20
reasons managers like to withhold unrestricted, readily=20
distributable earnings from shareholders - to expand the=20
corporate empire over which the managers rule, to operate from a=20
position of exceptional financial comfort, etc.  But we believe=20
there is only one valid reason for retention.  Unrestricted=20
earnings should be retained only when there is a reasonable=20
prospect - backed preferably by historical evidence or, when=20
appropriate, by a thoughtful analysis of the future - that <i>for=20
every dollar retained by the corporation, at least one dollar of=20
market value will be created for owners.</i>  This will happen only=20
if the capital retained produces incremental earnings equal to,=20
or above, those generally available to investors.

     To illustrate, let=92s assume that an investor owns a risk-
free 10% perpetual bond with one very unusual feature.  Each year=20
the investor can elect either to take his 10% coupon in cash, or=20
to reinvest the coupon in more 10% bonds with identical terms;=20
i.e., a perpetual life and coupons offering the same cash-or-
reinvest option.  If, in any given year, the prevailing interest=20
rate on long-term, risk-free bonds is 5%, it would be foolish for=20
the investor to take his coupon in cash since the 10% bonds he=20
could instead choose would be worth considerably more than 100=20
cents on the dollar.  Under these circumstances, the investor=20
wanting to get his hands on cash should take his coupon in=20
additional bonds and then immediately sell them.  By doing that,=20
he would realize more cash than if he had taken his coupon=20
directly in cash.  Assuming all bonds were held by rational=20
investors, no one would opt for cash in an era of 5% interest=20
rates, not even those bondholders needing cash for living=20
purposes.

     If, however, interest rates were 15%, no rational investor=20
would want his money invested for him at 10%.  Instead, the=20
investor would choose to take his coupon in cash, even if his=20
personal cash needs were nil.  The opposite course - reinvestment=20
of the coupon - would give an investor additional bonds with=20
market value far less than the cash he could have elected.  If he=20
should want 10% bonds, he can simply take the cash received=20
and buy them in the market, where they will be available at a=20
large discount.

     An analysis similar to that made by our hypothetical=20
bondholder is appropriate for owners in thinking about whether a=20
company=92s unrestricted earnings should be retained or paid out. =20
Of course, the analysis is much more difficult and subject to=20
error because the rate earned on reinvested earnings is not a=20
contractual figure, as in our bond case, but rather a fluctuating=20
figure.  Owners must guess as to what the rate will average over=20
the intermediate future.  However, once an informed guess is=20
made, the rest of the analysis is simple: you should wish your=20
earnings to be reinvested if they can be expected to earn high=20
returns, and you should wish them paid to you if low returns are=20
the likely outcome of reinvestment.

     Many corporate managers reason very much along these lines=20
in determining whether subsidiaries should distribute earnings to=20
their parent company.  At that level,. the managers have no=20
trouble thinking like intelligent owners.  But payout decisions=20
at the parent company level often are a different story.  Here=20
managers frequently have trouble putting themselves in the shoes=20
of their shareholder-owners.

     With this schizoid approach, the CEO of a multi-divisional=20
company will instruct Subsidiary A, whose earnings on incremental=20
capital may be expected to average 5%, to distribute all=20
available earnings in order that they may be invested in=20
Subsidiary B, whose earnings on incremental capital are expected=20
to be 15%.  The CEO=92s business school oath will allow no lesser=20
behavior.  But if his own long-term record with incremental=20
capital is 5% - and market rates are 10% - he is likely to impose=20
a dividend policy on shareholders of the parent company that=20
merely follows some historical or industry-wide payout pattern. =20
Furthermore, he will expect managers of subsidiaries to give him=20
a full account as to why it makes sense for earnings to be=20
retained in their operations rather than distributed to the=20
parent-owner.  But seldom will he supply his owners with a=20
similar analysis pertaining to the whole company.

     In judging whether managers should retain earnings,=20
shareholders should not simply compare total incremental earnings=20
in recent years to total incremental capital because that=20
relationship may be distorted by what is going on in a core=20
business.  During an inflationary period, companies with a core=20
business characterized by extraordinary economics can use small=20
amounts of incremental capital in that business at very high=20
rates of return (as was discussed in last year=92s section on=20
Goodwill).  But, unless they are experiencing tremendous unit=20
growth, outstanding businesses by definition generate large=20
amounts of excess cash.  If a company sinks most of this money in=20
other businesses that earn low returns, the company=92s overall=20
return on retained capital may nevertheless appear excellent=20
because of the extraordinary returns being earned by the portion=20
of earnings incrementally invested in the core business.  The=20
situation is analogous to a Pro-Am golf event: even if all of the=20
amateurs are hopeless duffers, the team=92s best-ball score will be=20
respectable because of the dominating skills of the professional.

     Many corporations that consistently show good returns both=20
on equity and on overall incremental capital have, indeed,=20
employed a large portion of their retained earnings on an=20
economically unattractive, even disastrous, basis.  Their=20
marvelous core businesses, however, whose earnings grow year=20
after year, camouflage repeated failures in capital allocation=20
elsewhere (usually involving high-priced acquisitions of=20
businesses that have inherently mediocre economics).  The=20
managers at fault periodically report on the lessons they have=20
learned from the latest disappointment.  They then usually seek=20
out future lessons. (Failure seems to go to their heads.)

     In such cases, shareholders would be far better off if=20
earnings were retained only to expand the high-return business,=20
with the balance paid in dividends or used to repurchase stock=20
(an action that increases the owners=92 interest in the exceptional=20
business while sparing them participation in subpar businesses). =20
Managers of high-return businesses who consistently employ much=20
of the cash thrown off by those businesses in other ventures with=20
low returns should be held to account for those allocation=20
decisions, regardless of how profitable the overall enterprise=20
is.

     Nothing in this discussion is intended to argue for=20
dividends that bounce around from quarter to quarter with each=20
wiggle in earnings or in investment opportunities.  Shareholders=20
of public corporations understandably prefer that dividends be=20
consistent and predictable.  Payments, therefore, should reflect=20
long-term expectations for both earnings and returns on=20
incremental capital.  Since the long-term corporate outlook=20
changes only infrequently, dividend patterns should change no=20
more often.  But over time distributable earnings that have been=20
withheld by managers should earn their keep.  If earnings have=20
been unwisely retained, it is likely that managers, too, have=20
been unwisely retained.

     Let=92s now turn to Berkshire Hathaway and examine how these=20
dividend principles apply to it.  Historically, Berkshire has=20
earned well over market rates on retained earnings, thereby=20
creating over one dollar of market value for every dollar=20
retained.  Under such circumstances, any distribution would have=20
been contrary to the financial interest of shareholders, large or=20
small.

     In fact, significant distributions in the early years might=20
have been disastrous, as a review of our starting position will=20
show you.  Charlie and I then controlled and managed three=20
companies, Berkshire Hathaway Inc., Diversified Retailing=20
Company, Inc., and Blue Chip Stamps (all now merged into our=20
present operation).  Blue Chip paid only a small dividend,=20
Berkshire and DRC paid nothing.  If, instead, the companies had=20
paid out their entire earnings, we almost certainly would have no=20
earnings at all now - and perhaps no capital as well.  The three=20
companies each originally made their money from a single=20
business: (1) textiles at Berkshire; (2) department stores at=20
Diversified; and (3) trading stamps at Blue Chip.  These=20
cornerstone businesses (carefully chosen, it should be noted, by=20
your Chairman and Vice Chairman) have, respectively, (1) survived=20
but earned almost nothing, (2) shriveled in size while incurring=20
large losses, and (3) shrunk in sales volume to about 5% its size=20
at the time of our entry.  (Who says =93you can=92t lose =91em all=94?)=20
Only by committing available funds to much better businesses were=20
we able to overcome these origins. (It=92s been like overcoming a=20
misspent youth.) Clearly, diversification has served us well.

     We expect to continue to diversify while also supporting the=20
growth of current operations though, as we=92ve pointed out, our=20
returns from these efforts will surely be below our historical=20
returns.  But as long as prospective returns are above the rate=20
required to produce a dollar of market value per dollar retained,=20
we will continue to retain all earnings.  Should our estimate of=20
future returns fall below that point, we will distribute all=20
unrestricted earnings that we believe can not be effectively=20
used.  In making that judgment, we will look at both our=20
historical record and our prospects.  Because our year-to-year=20
results are inherently volatile, we believe a five-year rolling=20
average to be appropriate for judging the historical record.

     Our present plan is to use our retained earnings to further=20
build the capital of our insurance companies.  Most of our=20
competitors are in weakened financial condition and reluctant to=20
expand substantially.  Yet large premium-volume gains for the=20
industry are imminent, amounting probably to well over $15=20
billion in 1985 versus less than $5 billion in 1983.  These=20
circumstances could produce major amounts of profitable business=20
for us.  Of course, this result is no sure thing, but prospects=20
for it are far better than they have been for many years.

<b>Miscellaneous</b>

     This is the spot where each year I run my small =93business=20
wanted=94 ad.  In 1984 John Loomis, one of our particularly=20
knowledgeable and alert shareholders, came up with a company that=20
met all of our tests.  We immediately pursued this idea, and only=20
a chance complication prevented a deal.  Since our ad is pulling,=20
we will repeat it in precisely last year=92s form:

     We prefer:
     (1) large purchases (at least $5 million of after-tax=20
         earnings),
     (2) demonstrated consistent earning power (future=20
         projections are of little interest to us, nor are=20
         =93turn-around=94 situations),
     (3) businesses earning good returns on equity while=20
         employing little or no debt,
     (4) management in place (we can=92t supply it),
     (5) simple businesses (if there=92s lots of technology, we=20
         won=92t understand it),
     (6) an offering price (we don=92t want to waste our time or=20
         that of the seller by talking, even preliminarily,=20
         about a transaction when price is unknown).

     We will not engage in unfriendly takeovers.  We can promise=20
complete confidentiality and a very fast answer - customarily=20
within five minutes - as to whether we=92re interested.  We prefer=20
to buy for cash, but will consider issuance of stock when we=20
receive as much in intrinsic business value as we give.  We=20
invite potential sellers to check us out by contacting people=20
with whom we have done business in the past.  For the right=20
business - and the right people - we can provide a good home.

