                           *  *  *

     Our annual meeting will be on May 21, 1985 in Omaha, and I=20
hope that you attend.  Many annual meetings are a waste of time,=20
both for shareholders and for management.  Sometimes that is true=20
because management is reluctant to open up on matters of business=20
substance.  More often a nonproductive session is the fault of=20
shareholder participants who are more concerned about their own=20
moment on stage than they are about the affairs of the=20
corporation.  What should be a forum for business discussion=20
becomes a forum for theatrics, spleen-venting and advocacy of=20
issues. (The deal is irresistible: for the price of one share you=20
get to tell a captive audience your ideas as to how the world=20
should be run.) Under such circumstances, the quality of the=20
meeting often deteriorates from year to year as the antics of=20
those interested in themselves discourage attendance by those=20
interested in the business.

     Berkshire=92s meetings are a different story.  The number of=20
shareholders attending grows a bit each year and we have yet to=20
experience a silly question or an ego-inspired commentary. =20
Instead, we get a wide variety of thoughtful questions about the=20
business.  Because the annual meeting is the time and place for=20
these, Charlie and I are happy to answer them all, no matter how=20
long it takes. (We cannot, however, respond to written or phoned=20
questions at other times of the year; one-person-at-a time=20
reporting is a poor use of management time in a company with 3000=20
shareholders.) The only business matters that are off limits at=20
the annual meeting are those about which candor might cost our=20
company real money.  Our activities in securities would be the=20
main example.

     We always have bragged a bit on these pages about the=20
quality of our shareholder-partners.  Come to the annual meeting=20
and you will see why.  Out-of-towners should schedule a stop at=20
Nebraska Furniture Mart.  If you make some purchases, you=92ll save=20
far more than enough to pay for your trip, and you=92ll enjoy the=20
